UID:019d2732-52a9-a9c4-1341-06b9de4cac7b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Angela Sanchez of LatinX in Animation and Austin Faber of Blac
 k N’ Animated discuss the past\, present\, and future of their communiti
 es’ voices in animated film.\n\nFriday\, May 15\, 2026 at 12 pm\n\nLocat
 ion: Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\n\nBlack N’ Anima
 ted Director Austin Faber and LatinX in Animation Development and Strategy
  Consultant Angela M. Sanchez discuss the history of their communities in 
 animated films\, the significance of authentic representation in animation
 \, how to support underrepresented animators\, and more.\nMarge Dean\, Hea
 d of Animation Studio at Skybound Entertainment\, moderates.
LOCATION:Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/community-conversatio
 n-representation-in-animation-019d2732-52a9-a9c4-1341-06b9de4cac7b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Wages of Fear in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T163000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019be23e-8e89-d267-ff1c-c919de073eae@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 15\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 35m\n\n1953 | 155 min. | France\, Italy 
 | Black-and-White | French with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nD
 IRECTED BY: Henri-Georges Clouzot \nWRITTEN BY: Henri-Georges Clouzot\, J
 érôme Géronimi \nWITH: Yves Montand\, Charles Vanel\, Peter van Eyck\, 
 Folco Lulli\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWriter-director Henri-Georges Clouzot (
 Diabolique\, 1955) adapted Georges Arnaud’s 1950 novel for this intense 
 thriller\, which won the Golden Bear at the Berlin International Film Fest
 ival and the Palme d’Or at Cannes. Yves Montand plays Mario\, a playboy 
 stuck in a remote South American town. Down on his luck\, he joins three o
 ther desperate men in a death-defying task: driving two trucks loaded with
  nitroglycerine over treacherous mountain roads to put out an oil well fir
 e. Bong Joon Ho told an interviewer in 2020 that this was one of two films
  he saw when he was nine that inspired him to become a film director\; the
  other was Psycho (1960). William Friedkin remade the story in English in 
 1977 as Sorcerer.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/an-angel-at-my-table-
 019be23e-8e89-d267-ff1c-c919de073eae
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:I Know Where I’m Going!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T213000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c0657-44a2-f54a-81a0-551563c99dd2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 15\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 32m\n\n1945 | 92 min. | UK | Black-and-W
 hite | English\, Scottish Gaelic | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: 
 Michael Powell\, Emeric Pressburger\nWITH: Wendy Hiller\, Roger Livesey\, 
 Pamela Brown\, Finlay Currie\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFor all of her 30 year
 s\, nothing has stopped headstrong Joan Webster (Wendy Hiller) and her per
 sistent march toward her goals. Her latest: holy matrimony with a business
  magnate twice her age\, whom she hopes to wed in the Scottish Hebrides. W
 hen relentless storms prevent her from completing her journey\, she succum
 bs to the charms of the place\, and of one local in particular\, who might
  be the first person ever to slow her down. \nWritten in four days and pra
 ised by Raymond Chandler for its evocation of the senses\, this romantic t
 ale reaches folkloric heights thanks to detailed location shooting and the
  unmatched world-building of production designer Alfred Junge.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/i-know-where-im-going
 -019c0657-44a2-f54a-81a0-551563c99dd2
END:VEVENT
