 to the Coen Brothers\,” Bong Joon Ho says in the catalog for the exhibit
 ion Director’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho. “Their movies are full of mi
 sunderstandings and misinterpretations.” Set in 1941\, Barton Fink is th
 e story of the titular East Coast transplant (played by John Turturro) who
  abandons his playwrighting career to try his hand in Hollywood. During a 
 bout of writer’s block in his dilapidated hotel\, Fink becomes acquainte
 d with his noisy neighbor (John Goodman)\, an insurance salesman whose aff
 able appearance belies his true nature. The fourth feature by the Coens wa
 s also the first to win all three major prizes—Best Director\, Best Acto
 r\, and the Palme d’Or—at the Cannes Film Festival. The film was nomin
 ated at the 64th Academy Awards for Supporting Actor (Michael Lerner)\, Ar
 t Direction\, and Costume Design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/barton-fink-019be23f-
 4f4e-d945-a75a-c25d2a4f792e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dersu Uzala in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260522T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260522T203000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c065c-245c-46fd-e698-58e38fa15a96@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Anne McKnight\, associate professor of Japanese and
  comparative literature\n\nFriday\, May 22\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: 
 Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 22m\n\n1975 | 142 min. | 
 Soviet Union/Japan | Color | Russian\, Chinese with English subtitles | No
 t Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\,
  Yuriy Nagibin \nWITH: Maksim Munzuk\, Yuri Solomin\, Mikhail Bychkov\, Vl
 adimir Khrulyov \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nCaptain Arsenyev (Yuri Solomin) leads a group of Russian s
 oldiers in his expedition of the Russian Far East region in the early 1900
 s\, striking up an endearing friendship with a nomad hunter\, Dersu Uzala 
 (Maksim Munzuk) in the process. Based on a memoir by Vladimir Klavdiyevich
  Arsenyev\, a renowned Russian explorer whose research spanned regions of 
 the Far East\, Kurosawa’s epic drama contemplates the relationships betw
 een humanity\, nature\, and the spiritual realm. The vastness of the Siber
 ian forests\, previously unfamiliar to the Japanese master\, underscores t
 he immense power of nature and the beauty found in simplicity.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/dersu-uzala-019c065c-
 245c-46fd-e698-58e38fa15a96
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kiki’s Delivery Service
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T121500
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019be21f-5322-96c7-9e6c-1577c53dc3f3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 23\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 42m\n\n1989 | 102 min. | Japan | Color |
  English (Dubbed) | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWRITTEN 
 BY: Eiko Kadono\, Hayao Miyazaki  \nWITH: Kirsten Dunst\, Janeane Garofalo
 \, Phil Hartman\, Debbie Reynolds\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA witch film like
  few others\, Kiki’s Delivery Service is a warm-hearted story that follo
 ws a girl entering adulthood and discovering her capabilities\, while\, re
 freshingly\, refusing romance as a central plot device. Following traditio
 n\, on the night of the full moon after she turns 13\, Kiki must take flig
 ht on her broom and leave her family behind to start a new life in the cha
 rming port city of Koriko\, where she will learn to use her powers. With a
  wise-cracking black cat named Jiji by her side\, enterprising Kiki quickl
 y becomes a local phenomenon as she strives to find her community and hers
 elf.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/kikis-delivery-servic
 e-019be21f-5322-96c7-9e6c-1577c53dc3f3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Yellow Balloon
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T160000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c53be-67ce-e9e2-af0c-cb09a7d68c53@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 23\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1953 | 79 min. | UK | Black-and
 -White. English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: J. Lee Thompson \nWRITT
 EN BY: Anne Burnaby\, J. Lee Thompson \nWITH: Andrew Ray\, Kenneth More\,
  Kathleen Ryan\, Bernard Lee\n\nType: Screenings\n\nIn the bombed-out ruin
 s of London\, a boy accidentally causes his friend’s death and is blackm
 ailed by a criminal into aiding a robbery. This unflinching drama marked f
 ilmmaker J. Lee Thompson’s breakthrough\, showcasing a bold\, gritty ima
 gination that would later be fully realized in his harrowing Cape Fear (19
 62). Slapped with the adults-only X certificate on its initial release—m
 eaning lead actor Andrew Ray wasn’t allowed to see it—the film’s mos
 t disturbing scenes of childhood peril still resonate with the same tensio
 n and terror as they did more than 70 years ago.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-yellow-balloon-01
 9c53be-67ce-e9e2-af0c-cb09a7d68c53
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ran (Chaos) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260523T203000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c065c-c773-0043-1e8d-ffaa8ec242b0@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Collection and Preservation Executive Vice 
 President Matt Severson\n\nSaturday\, May 23\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation
 : Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 40m\n\n1985 | 160 min. 
 | Japan | Color | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRE
 CTED BY: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Hideo Oguni\, Masa
 to Ide \nBASED ON A PLAY BY: William Shakespeare \nWITH: Tatsuya Nakadai
 \, Akira Terao\, Jinpachi Nezu\, Daisuke Ryu\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe in
 tricate family dynamics of Shakespeare’s King Lear are transposed to 16t
 h-century Japan—a century of social unrest and near-continuous civil war
 s—in this late-career masterpiece from the 75-year-old filmmaker. After 
 more than a decade of meticulous planning\, including gorgeous storyboards
  painted by the filmmaker himself\, Kurosawa’s sweeping psychological ep
 ic was made possible due to the financial support of the same adventurous 
 financier who backed Spanish-Mexican filmmaker Luis Buñuel’s final five
  films. At the time the most expensive movie ever made in Japan\, Ran also
  marks the director’s first and only Best Directing nomination at the Os
 cars\; costume designer Emi Wada won for Best Costume Design\; Yoshio Mura
 ki and Shinobu Muraki were nominated for Art Direction\; and Takao Saito\,
  Masaharu Ueda\, and Asakazu Nakai were nominated for Cinematography.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/ran-chaos-in-35-mm-01
 9c065c-c773-0043-1e8d-ffaa8ec242b0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Erin Brockovich in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T160000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019be228-9540-a09b-9917-1ac6d9d43cd6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 24\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of 
 Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 11m\n\n2000 | 131 min. | USA | Color | Engl
 ish | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Steven Soderbergh \nWRITTEN BY: Susa
 nnah Grant \nWITH: Julia Roberts\, Albert Finney\, Aaron Eckhart\, Marg He
 lgenberger\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJulia Roberts won the Best Actress Oscar
  for her commanding performance as the real-life legal assistant taking on
  a power company that poisoned a small California town. The film received 
 nominations for Best Picture (Danny DeVito\, Michael Shamberg\, and Stacey
  Sher\, producers)\, Steven Soderbergh’s direction (he won that same yea
 r for Traffic\, in competition with himself)\, Susannah Grant’s original
  screenplay\, and the supporting performance of Albert Finney as attorney 
 Ed Masry\, part of the expert cast chosen by Soderbergh and casting direct
 or Margery Simkin. Additional inspired casting decisions came via Marg Hel
 genberger as an ailing plaintiff and Aaron Eckhart as Roberts’s biker lo
 ve interest.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/erin-brockovich-019be
 228-9540-a09b-9917-1ac6d9d43cd6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Brighton Rock in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T160000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c065d-45d5-fcd1-36cd-b9c126339a8f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 24\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 32m\n\n1948 | 92 min. | UK | Black-and-W
 hite | English | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Boulting \nWRITTEN 
 BY: Graham Greene\, Terence Rattigan  \nBASED ON A NOVEL BY: Graham Green
 e \nWITH: Richard Attenborough\, Hermione Baddeley\, William Hartnell\, H
 arcourt Williams\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA young Richard Attenborough\, emb
 odying the quintessential smooth-talking\, suave-dressing gent of British 
 cinema\, plays Pinkie\, a hoodlum forced to marry the witness to a murder 
 he committed. The script\, based on English writer Graham Greene’s novel
 \, was cowritten by Greene and Terence Rattigan\, one of the country’s m
 ost popular dramatists. \nProduced by the Boulting brothers—John would d
 irect\, Roy would produce—and shot with an expressionistic\, noir style 
 by Harry Waxman (Sapphire\, 1959) on location in the seaside town of Brigh
 ton\, the picture was a box office success and a critical flop\, with its 
 more violent sequences causing moral panic throughout the country—the fi
 lm was even banned in parts of Wales.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/brighton-rock-019c065
 d-45d5-fcd1-36cd-b9c126339a8f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kagemusha (The Shadow Warrior) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260524T203000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019c065d-e0d2-dc4e-d8c9-9f37095148b3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Kerim Yasar\, associate professor of East Asian lan
 guages and cultures\n\nSunday\, May 24\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Acad
 emy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 42m\n\n1980 | 162 min. | Japa
 n | Color | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated PG | 35mm.\nDIRECTED B
 Y: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Masato Ide \nWITH: Tatsu
 ya Nakadai\, Ken’ichi Hagiwara\, Kota Yui\, Tsutomu Yamazaki \nNew prin
 t courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKurosawa fa
 ced financial challenges in the 1970s\, including two unsuccessful endeavo
 rs for Hollywood projects that earned him a reputation for being difficult
  to work with. While his regular collaborators were disengaged\, emerging 
 filmmakers in Hollywood expressed their admiration for the director\, incl
 uding George Lucas. With Lucas as executive producer\, Kagemusha resurrect
 ed Kurosawa from this dark period\, illuminating his keen artistry in orch
 estrating a massive period piece that infuses picturesque shots and a medi
 tative approach to storytelling with a sense of irony.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/kagemusha-019c065d-e0
 d2-dc4e-d8c9-9f37095148b3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cure in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260525T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260525T163000
DTSTAMP:20260510T084150Z
UID:019be23f-e508-83c4-5f7c-abd6ae912964@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 25\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 51m\n\n1997 | 111 min. | Japan | Color |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY
 : Kiyoshi Kurosawa  \nWITH: Kôji Yakusho\, Masato Hagiwara\, Tsuyoshi Uj
 iki\, Yukijiro Hotaru\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis brooding psychological c
 rime thriller introduced writer-director Kiyoshi Kurosawa to an internatio
 nal audience and was an influence on the wave of Japanese horror films tha
 t were popular in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Koji Yakusho stars as Ta
 kabe\, a Tokyo detective assigned to a string of violent murders carried o
 ut by people with seemingly no motive. While struggling to cope with his w
 ife’s schizophrenia\, Takabe find his investigation leading him to the e
 nigmatic Mamiya (Masato Hagiwara)\, a former psychology student dealing wi
 th amnesia. Kurosawa uses thriller and horror elements to create an explor
 ation of the existential that is fraught with tension.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/cure-019be23f-e508-83
 c4-5f7c-abd6ae912964
END:VEVENT
